    faq: [
      { q: 'What is WCAG color contrast?', a: 'WCAG (Web Content Accessibility Guidelines) defines minimum contrast ratios between foreground text and background color to ensure readability for users with low vision or color blindness. WCAG 2.1 Level AA requires 4.5:1 for normal-sized text (below 18pt or 14pt bold) and 3:1 for large text. Most legal accessibility standards worldwide — US ADA, EU EN 301 549, UK Equality Act — reference WCAG AA as the required level.' },
      { q: 'What is the difference between WCAG AA and AAA?', a: 'WCAG AA (Level 2) is the legally mandated standard in most countries for public-facing websites. It requires 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for large text. WCAG AAA (Level 3) is the enhanced standard requiring 7:1 for normal text and 4.5:1 for large text. Most design teams target AA throughout and apply AAA selectively to primary reading content like body copy and legal text.' },
      { q: 'How is contrast ratio calculated?', a: 'Contrast ratio = (L1 + 0.05) / (L2 + 0.05), where L1 is the relative luminance of the lighter color and L2 is the luminance of the darker. Relative luminance linearizes the sRGB color space using a gamma correction formula. The scale runs from 1:1 (identical colors, no contrast) to 21:1 (black #000000 on white #FFFFFF, maximum contrast). A ratio of 4.5:1 means the lighter color is 4.5× more luminous.' },
      { q: 'Does this apply to icons?', a: 'Yes. WCAG 2.1 Success Criterion 1.4.11 (Non-text Contrast) requires a 3:1 minimum contrast ratio for user interface components: icons that convey meaning, input field borders (to distinguish from the background), focus indicators (tab focus rings), toggle switch boundaries, and chart elements. The same luminance formula applies. Purely decorative images and disabled UI components are exempt.' },
      { q: 'What contrast ratio does Google Material Design use?', a: 'Google Material Design 3 (Material You) requires a minimum 4.5:1 contrast for body and label text — matching WCAG AA exactly. Its dynamic color system generates color schemes that automatically satisfy this threshold. Apple\'s Human Interface Guidelines also recommend 4.5:1 minimum. Note: WCAG 3.0 (in active development) plans to replace the current luminance-ratio formula with APCA (Advanced Perceptual Contrast Algorithm), which better models human visual perception.' },
      { q: 'What counts as large text in WCAG?', a: 'Large text is at least 18pt (approximately 24px) at normal weight, or 14pt (approximately 18.66px) at bold weight. Large text only needs a 3:1 contrast ratio for WCAG AA and 4.5:1 for AAA — the lower threshold exists because larger letterforms remain legible at lower contrast. If your font size sits between these thresholds, check whether it is rendered bold to determine which requirement applies.' },
      { q: 'Does this contrast checker send my colors to a server?', a: 'No. The contrast ratio is calculated entirely in your browser using JavaScript. Nothing is uploaded, logged, or stored — not even temporarily. It is safe to check colors from unreleased designs, internal brand palettes, or client projects. The tool works fully offline once the page is loaded.' },
    ],
