    lightColor: '#fff1ee',
    metaTitle: 'Payroll Breakdown Calculator — Free Net Pay Calculator Online',
    metaDescription: 'Calculate net pay from gross salary. Add earnings components, deductions, and tax withholding to get a full payroll breakdown. Free, no login, instant results.',
    keywords: ['payroll breakdown calculator', 'net pay calculator free', 'gross to net salary calculator', 'payroll calculator online', 'salary net pay calculator', 'payroll deductions calculator'],
    h1: 'Free Payroll Breakdown Calculator — Gross to Net Pay',
    intro: 'A payroll breakdown calculator computes an employee\'s net pay by starting with gross salary and subtracting all earnings adjustments, pre-tax deductions (health insurance, pension), income tax withholding, and post-tax deductions. This tool gives a line-by-line payslip breakdown for any salary structure.',
    howToUse: [
      { step: 'Enter gross salary', desc: 'Input the employee\'s monthly or annual gross salary.' },
      { step: 'Add earnings and deductions', desc: 'Add allowances (housing, transport) and deductions (pension, health, tax). Set each as fixed amount or percentage of gross.' },
      { step: 'See net pay breakdown', desc: 'The calculator shows total earnings, total deductions, and final net pay — ready to use as a payslip.' },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: 'What is the difference between gross and net pay?', a: 'Gross pay is the total compensation agreed in the employment contract before any deductions — base salary plus bonuses, allowances, and overtime pay. Net pay (take-home pay) is the amount deposited into the employee\'s account after income tax withholding, employee pension contributions, health insurance premiums, and any other authorized pre-tax and post-tax deductions are subtracted from gross.' },
      { q: 'What are pre-tax deductions?', a: 'Pre-tax deductions are subtracted from gross pay before income tax is calculated, reducing the taxable base. Common examples: 401k/pension contributions, employer-sponsored health insurance premiums, HSA and FSA contributions, and commuter benefits. Because they reduce taxable income, pre-tax deductions save the employee both income tax and, in some cases, FICA payroll tax on the deducted amount.' },
      { q: 'What is a payslip?', a: 'A payslip (or pay stub) is a document provided with each pay period showing: gross pay, all deductions itemized line-by-line (income tax, pension, health insurance, loan repayments), employer contributions, and final net pay. Most countries legally require employers to provide payslips — the UK Employment Rights Act 1996 requires them from day one. Payslips are also essential for visa applications, mortgage approvals, and benefits claims.' },
      { q: 'How do percentage-based components work?', a: 'A component set as "% of gross" calculates its value as a proportion of the gross salary, adjusting automatically when the salary changes. For example, a 10% housing allowance on a $3,000 gross adds $300; on a $4,000 gross it adds $400. Percentage-based components are common for housing allowance, transport allowance, and performance bonuses in structured payroll systems.' },
      { q: 'Can I use this for any country?', a: 'Yes. The calculator is deliberately country-neutral — it does not apply specific tax brackets, social security rates, or statutory deduction rules automatically. You manually enter tax amounts or rates, pre-tax deduction percentages, and post-tax deductions. This makes it equally usable for UK PAYE, US federal/state withholding, Indian TDS, Bangladesh payroll, UAE salary structures, or any other jurisdiction.' },
      { q: 'How is net pay calculated?', a: 'Net pay is computed in a fixed sequence: gross earnings minus pre-tax deductions equals taxable income; taxable income minus income-tax withholding equals pay after tax; pay after tax minus post-tax deductions equals net pay. The order matters — pre-tax deductions reduce taxable income before withholding is applied, so classifying each deduction correctly produces a materially different (and accurate) net figure.' },
      { q: 'Is my salary data sent to a server?', a: 'No. The entire payroll breakdown is calculated in your browser with JavaScript. No salary figures, deduction amounts, or tax rates are uploaded, logged, or stored. The calculator works fully offline once the page is loaded.' },
    ],
