    commonErrors: [
      { error: 'Using annual salary without converting to an hourly rate', code: '$52,000/year entered as the hourly rate', fix: 'Convert annual salary to hourly first: divide by 2,080 (52 weeks × 40 hours). A $52,000 salary = $25/hour regular rate, $37.50/hour overtime. Entering $52,000 as the hourly rate produces a wildly inflated figure.' },
      { error: 'Applying the overtime rate to all hours instead of only the excess', code: 'Total pay = 50 hours × $30 (1.5×) overtime rate', fix: 'Only hours beyond the threshold are paid at the overtime rate. Regular hours (typically the first 40) are paid at the base rate: 40 × $20 + 10 × $30 = $800 + $300 = $1,100. Applying 1.5× to all 50 hours overpays by $200.' },
      { error: 'Using take-home (net) hourly rate instead of gross as the overtime base', code: 'Overtime base = $17/hr net after deductions', fix: 'Overtime is always calculated on the gross regular rate before tax, National Insurance, or any deductions. Use the gross pre-deduction hourly rate as your input.' },
      { error: 'Confusing weekly overtime (FLSA) with daily overtime (California)', code: 'Expecting 1.5× after 8 hours/day under federal law', fix: 'The FLSA sets no daily overtime threshold — it triggers at 40 hours/week. California additionally requires 1.5× after 8 hours in a single day and 2× after 12 hours. Select the correct jurisdiction rule for your employee.' },
      { error: 'Omitting non-discretionary bonuses from the regular rate', code: 'Regular rate = base hourly only, ignoring a $500 production bonus', fix: 'Under FLSA, non-discretionary bonuses (production bonuses, shift differentials, attendance bonuses) must be included when calculating the regular rate. Add the bonus to total pay for the period, then divide by total hours to get the true regular rate before applying the 1.5× multiplier.' },
      { error: 'Assuming salaried employees are automatically exempt from overtime', code: 'No overtime calculated because employee is salaried', fix: 'Salary alone does not determine FLSA exemption. Employees must meet both a salary threshold ($684/week as of 2024) AND a duties test (executive, administrative, professional). Salaried non-exempt employees earn overtime on their effective hourly rate (weekly salary ÷ hours worked).' },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: 'What is overtime pay?', a: 'Overtime pay is a higher rate paid for hours worked beyond a defined threshold — typically 40 hours/week in the US (FLSA) or 8 hours/day in some jurisdictions. The US federal minimum is 1.5× regular rate; some states and countries mandate 2×.' },
      { q: 'What is the FLSA overtime rule?', a: 'The Fair Labor Standards Act (FLSA) requires non-exempt employees to be paid at least 1.5× their regular rate for hours exceeding 40 per workweek. Exempt employees (managers, professionals earning above a threshold) are not covered.' },
      { q: 'How do I calculate time and a half?', a: 'Time and a half = regular hourly rate × 1.5. For a $20/hour regular rate: overtime rate = $30/hour. For 10 overtime hours: 10 × $30 = $300 in overtime pay, plus $800 regular pay (40 hours × $20) = $1,100 total gross for the week. To find the hourly rate from annual salary: divide by 2,080 (52 weeks × 40 hours). A $52,000/year salary = $25/hour regular, $37.50/hour overtime.' },
      { q: 'Is overtime calculated on base salary or total compensation?', a: 'Under FLSA, the regular rate for overtime includes most forms of compensation — base salary, shift differentials, and non-discretionary bonuses. Discretionary bonuses, gifts, and expense reimbursements are excluded.' },
      { q: 'What is double time?', a: 'Double time is 2× the regular hourly rate, typically paid for hours beyond 12 in a day or on holidays. California mandates double time for hours over 12 in a workday. It is not required under federal law but is common in union agreements.' },
      { q: 'Does overtime apply to salaried employees?', a: 'Not automatically. Under the FLSA, salaried employees are exempt from overtime only if they meet both a salary threshold ($684/week as of 2024) and a duties test (executive, administrative, or professional role). Salaried employees who do not meet both criteria are non-exempt and must receive 1.5× their effective hourly rate for hours over 40 per week.' },
      { q: 'Is my data sent to a server?', a: 'No. The overtime calculation runs entirely in your browser with JavaScript. No pay figures, hours, or personal data are uploaded, logged, or stored anywhere. You can use this calculator on confidential payroll data without any privacy concern.' },
    ],
